<h2>Goalkeeper Athletic Profile — What Actually Matters</h2>
<p>Goalkeeping is one of the few positions in sport where the athletic profile required is genuinely different from every other position on the field. Sprint speed — the dominant metric for outfield players — matters far less. What matters for a goalkeeper is a specific combination of measurements that determines how much of a goal they can cover and how quickly they can reach the extremes of it.</p>
<h3>Standing Reach — The Most Important Number</h3>
<p>Standing reach is how high a goalkeeper's fingertips can extend flat-footed. This determines the baseline coverage area before any jumping is required. The crossbar sits at 8 feet. For a 6'2" goalkeeper with proportional arm length, standing reach is typically around 7'8" to 7'10" — meaning a standard Vozinha-height keeper needs to jump approximately 2 to 3 feet to reach a ball aimed at the crossbar. Each additional inch of height reduces that required jump height and the reaction time window needed to execute it.</p>
<h3>Wingspan and Arm Length</h3>
<p>A goalkeeper's wingspan — the distance from fingertip to fingertip with arms extended — determines how wide they can reach in a single dive without leaving their feet. For a 6'2" goalkeeper, wingspan is typically between 6'3" and 6'6", depending on individual proportions. Each additional inch of wingspan extends the goalkeeper's effective coverage area without requiring footwork, which is critical since the fastest shots give keepers less than 250 milliseconds to react — not enough time to take a step.</p>
<p>Vozinha's viral save against Spain — diving to push a shot away with an extended right arm, then immediately rising to tip another header over the bar — demonstrated both his wingspan reach and his reactive athleticism in a single sequence that encapsulated everything the position requires.</p>
<h3>Vertical Jump</h3>
<p>Goalkeepers require vertical jump primarily for crosses and set pieces, where they must compete aerially with outfield players who may be taller or more physically imposing. A typical elite goalkeeper jumps approximately 25 to 32 inches on a standing vertical test. Vozinha's exact vertical is not publicly documented, but his in-game aerial performance against Spain — rising repeatedly to cut out crosses and claim balls at height — suggests it is solidly within the elite goalkeeper range for his age.</p>
